WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.300 align:start position:0%
There's something weird inside Mars. If
you took a slice of the surface, it

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:09.600 align:start position:0%
would look like this. Up here, by the
North Pole, Mars is all low flat lands.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.500 align:start position:0%
But down here, by the South Pole, it's
super bumpy. There are mountains three

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:18.500 align:start position:0%
times taller than Mount Everest and
craters nearly as wide as the moon.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.600 align:start position:0%
That's very different from the elevation
here on Earth. So scientists call this

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.100 align:start position:0%
divided geography the Mars dichotomy. So
to find out what's going on, they used

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.900 align:start position:0%
satellites [music] that were already
orbiting around Mars. And as Mars

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:33.400 align:start position:0%
travels in this oval path around the
sun, the sun's gravity slightly

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.900 align:start position:0%
stretches and compresses the planet,
which gently tugs on the satellites. So

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:41.400 align:start position:0%
by tracking tiny shifts in the
satellite's paths over many, many years,

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.800 align:start position:0%
scientists could map what's inside Mars.
And they found that the mantle, the

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.700 align:start position:0%
layer between the crust and the core, is
hundreds of degrees hotter in the

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.900 align:start position:0%
southern hemisphere than in the northern
one. So [music] the inside of Mars is

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.100 align:start position:0%
also divided, but we don't know which
came first. It could be that Mars's

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.700 align:start position:0%
insides have been heating up the south,
pushing the crust and creating the

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:04.300 align:start position:0%
mountains, or the thick mountains act
like a blanket heating up the inside, or

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.500 align:start position:0%
maybe an ancient asteroid smashed into
the northern hemisphere and is affecting

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:13.200 align:start position:0%
the planet inside and [music] out. But
what are these weird tubes on Mars? To

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.200 align:start position:0%
see what's inside, subscribe.

